       "24x24": "https://avatar-management--avatars.us-west-2.prod.public.atl-paas.net/6089a55c52a3d400690692f7/7a62c587-b0f1-4cd4-982c-21b3837dc64a/24",
       "32x32": "https://avatar-management--avatars.us-west-2.prod.public.atl-paas.net/6089a55c52a3d400690692f7/7a62c587-b0f1-4cd4-982c-21b3837dc64a/32",
       "48x48": "https://avatar-management--avatars.us-west-2.prod.public.atl-paas.net/6089a55c52a3d400690692f7/7a62c587-b0f1-4cd4-982c-21b3837dc64a/48"
      },
      "displayName": "Ralf Claussnitzer",
      "self": "https://fedora-repository.atlassian.net/rest/api/3/user?accountId=6089a55c52a3d400690692f7",
      "timeZone": "America/Halifax"
     },
     "body": "<p>Usage of `org.modeshape.jcr.security.AuthenticationProvider` can be found in the `fcrepo-auth-common module`. I assume this is the `f4-auth` JIRA-component? And additional interface type similar to this has to be defined in `fcrepo-kernel-api`, wrapping all of the used Modeshape types?</p>\n\n<p>This is bigger than the description suggests. The `fcrepo-auth-common` module depends on `modeshape-jcr` and `modeshape-web-jcr` (plus `fcrepo-kernel-modeshape` for testing).</p>\n\n<p>Not sure whether just wrapping all Modeshape types really accomplishes something. A naive approach would probably lead to a shallow copy of the Modeshape API itself. </p>",
     "created": "08/Feb/17 6:40 PM",
     "id": "18540",
     "jsdPublic": true,
     "self": "https://fedora-repository.atlassian.net/rest/api/3/issue/11657/comment/18540",
